// Essive / object-predicative `as` (D63 §5.3): the marker of the predicative complement in
// "identify / regard / describe / classify X AS Y". Like the other argmarkers it consumes the
// complement NP and yields a transparent `cat_pp_arg(prep_as)` (⟦·⟧ = Entity, sem `λy. y`); the
// essive verb frame `((S\NP)/cat_pp_arg(prep_as))/NP` (crates/eigenius-wordnet/src/convert.rs,
// `FrameKind::Essive`) binds it as the verb's third argument. Distinct `prep_as` so a plain
// transitive or single-PP verb does NOT accept a stray `as Y`.
resource lexicon:as_arg : lexicon:LexicalEntry {
    core:description = "grammatical: preposition/complementiser marking a role or comparison ('identified as a target').";
    lexicon:form     = "as";
    lexicon:cat      = type_expr( lexicon:fwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_pp_arg(lexicon:prep_as), lexicon:cat_np(lexicon:Entity, lexicon:num_any)) );
    lexicon:sem      = lexicon:argmarker_sem;
    lexicon:sem_type = type_expr( lexicon:Entity -> lexicon:Entity );
    lexicon:sense    = "as";
}

// Argument markers for every other preposition a SPECIALIST frame names (D97 decision 3): a word that
// governs one (`abide by`, `rely on`, `consist of`, `out of`) binds its object through it. Each is the
// `to_arg` shape. They do not put the word on the importers' closed-class list (decision 10, as
// proposed): `over`, `past` and `like` keep their content senses.
